{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01HVejuahAe69zFr48MgYDJx","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Contributing to Fromager\n2\t\n3\tFromager thrives on practical, well-tested contributions. This guide summarizes how to set up a workspace, follow our standards, and submit polished changes. Skim it once, keep it handy, and refer back whenever you are unsure.\n4\t\n5\t> **Note**: If you're using AI coding assistants, also see [AGENTS.md](AGENTS.md) for AI-optimized quick reference.\n6\t\n7\t## Table of Contents\n8\t\n9\t- [Quick Start](#quick-start)\n10\t- [Coding Standards](#coding-standards)\n11\t- [Testing](#testing)\n12\t- [Commit Guidelines](#commit-guidelines)\n13\t- [Before Submitting](#before-submitting)\n14\t- [Design Patterns Used in Fromager](#design-patterns-used-in-fromager)\n15\t- [Quick Reference](#quick-reference)\n16\t- [Getting Help](#getting-help)\n17\t- [Resources](#resources)\n18\t\n19\t---\n20\t\n21\t## Quick Start\n22\t\n23\t### Prerequisites\n24\t\n25\t- Python 3.12 or newer\n26\t\n27\t- `hatch` for environment and task management\n28\t\n29\t  ```bash\n30\t  pip install hatch\n31\t  # or\n32\t  pipx install hatch  # recommended\n33\t  ```\n34\t\n35\t### Initial Setup\n36\t\n37\t```bash\n38\t# 1. Fork the repository on GitHub\n39\t# 2. Clone your fork\n40\tgit clone https://github.com/<your-username>/fromager.git\n41\tcd fromager\n42\t\n43\t# 3. Add upstream remote\n44\tgit remote add upstream https://github.com/python-wheel-build/fromager.git\n45\t\n46\t# 4. Create development environment\n47\thatch env create\n48\t\n49\t# 5. Install pre-commit hooks (optional but recommended)\n50\thatch run lint:install-hooks\n51\t```\n52\t\n53\t### Contribution Workflow\n54\t\n55\t```bash\n56\t# 1. Sync with upstream\n57\tgit checkout main\n58\tgit fetch upstream\n59\tgit merge upstream/main\n60\t\n61\t# 2. Create a feature branch\n62\tgit checkout -b feat/<short-description>\n63\t\n64\t# 3. Make changes and test as you go\n65\thatch run test:test tests/test_<module>.py       # Test your specific changes\n66\t\n67\t# 4. Before committing, run full quality checks\n68\thatch run lint:precommit\n69\t\n70\t# 5. Commit using Conventional Commits\n71\tgit commit -m \"feat(scope): short summary\"\n72\t\n73\t# 6. Push to your fork\n74\tgit push origin feat/<short-description>\n75\t\n76\t# 7. Create a pull request on GitHub\n77\t```\n78\t\n79\t### Pre-commit Hook (Recommended)\n80\t\n81\tTo ensure quality checks run automatically before each commit, install the pre-commit hooks that run linting, mypy type checking, and commit message validation:\n82\t\n83\t```bash\n84\t# Install pre-commit hooks (run once after cloning)\n85\thatch run lint:install-hooks\n86\t\n87\t# The hooks automatically run linters before each commit and validate\n88\t# that commit messages follow Conventional Commits format.\n89\t\n90\t# If a hook fails, it will prevent the commit and show helpful messages.\n91\t# You can fix formatting issues automatically with:\n92\thatch run lint:fix\n93\t```\n94\t\n95\tThe pre-commit hooks prevent commits that would fail CI quality checks, and enforce that all commit messages follow the [Conventional Commits](https://www.conventionalcommits.org/) format documented in the [Commit Guidelines](#commit-guidelines) section.\n96\t\n97\t---\n98\t\n99\t## Coding Standards\n100\t\n101\t### Type Annotations\n102\t\n103\t- Every function (including tests) must annotate all parameters and return values.\n104\t- Use modern `X | None` syntax instead of `Optional[X]` (PEP 604).\n105\t- Prefer precise collection types (`list[str]`, `dict[str, int]`, etc.).\n106\t\n107\t```python\n108\tdef process(data: str | None, count: int = 0) -> dict[str, int]:\n109\t    \"\"\"Process the input and return aggregate counts.\"\"\"\n110\t    return {}\n111\t```\n112\t\n113\t### Code Quality and Formatting\n114\t\n115\t- Ruff enforces both formatting and linting.\n116\t- Run `hatch run lint:fix` to automatically format code.\n117\t- See [Quick Reference](#quick-reference) for additional commands.\n118\t\n119\t### Import Organization\n120\t\n121\t- **PEP 8: imports should be at the top**: All import statements must be placed at the top of the file, after module docstrings and before other code.\n122\t- **No local imports**: Do not place import statements inside functions, methods, or conditional blocks.\n123\t\n124\t### Documentation Expectations\n125\t\n126\t- Add a module docstring describing purpose and high-level behavior.\n127\t- Public functions and classes require docstrings that cover arguments, return values, and noteworthy behavior.\n128\t- Keep prose short and imperative; explain \"why\" decisions when the code itself cannot.\n129\t\n130\t```python\n131\tdef retry_on_exception(\n132\t    exceptions: tuple[type[Exception], ...],\n133\t    max_attempts: int = 5,\n134\t) -> typing.Callable:\n135\t    \"\"\"Retry decorated call on the provided exception types.\n136\t\n137\t    Args:\n138\t        exceptions: Exception types that trigger a retry.\n139\t        max_attempts: Maximum number of attempts.\n140\t\n141\t    Returns:\n142\t        Decorator function.\n143\t    \"\"\"\n144\t    ...\n145\t```\n146\t\n147\t### Commenting Guidelines\n148\t\n149\t- Write self-explanatory code; reserve comments for non-obvious intent or domain context.\n150\t- Capture reasoning, invariants, or unexpected trade-offs—never repeat the code literally.\n151\t\n152\t```python\n153\t# Bad - comment just repeats the code\n154\tx = x + 1  # increment x\n155\t\n156\t# Good - clear variable name makes the comment unnecessary\n157\ttotal_attempts += 1\n158\t\n159\t# Good - comment explains the reasoning behind the approach\n160\t# Exponential backoff avoids thundering herd when many jobs retry at once.\n161\twait_time = min(2**attempt + random.uniform(0, 1), max_backoff)\n162\t```\n163\t\n164\t### Logging\n165\t\n166\t- Use a module-level logger and the appropriate log level for the situation.\n167\t- When processing per-requirement work, wrap nested calls in `req_ctxvar_context()` so log records automatically include the package (and optional version). This keeps CLI logs searchable even when work runs in parallel.\n168\t\n169\t```python\n170\tlogger = logging.getLogger(__name__)\n171\t\n172\tdef sync_artifacts() -> None:\n173\t    logger.info(\"Starting artifact sync\")\n174\t    logger.debug(\"Artifacts queued: %s\", pending_jobs)\n175\t\n176\t# When processing a specific package, wrap calls to include package info in logs\n177\t# req: Requirement object for the package being processed\n178\t# version: Version string being built (optional)\n179\twith req_ctxvar_context(req, version):\n180\t    logger.info(\"Resolving build dependencies\")  # Logs will include package name\n181\t```\n182\t\n183\t### Error Handling\n184\t\n185\t- Raise specific exceptions with actionable messages.\n186\t- Chain exceptions (`raise ... from e`) so stack traces stay informative.\n187\t\n188\t```python\n189\ttry:\n190\t    result = process_data(file_path)\n191\texcept FileNotFoundError as err:\n192\t    raise ValueError(f\"Cannot load config at {file_path}\") from err\n193\t```\n194\t\n195\t---\n196\t\n197\t## Testing\n198\t\n199\t### Structure\n200\t\n201\t- Place tests under `tests/`.\n202\t- Name files `test_<module>.py` and functions `test_<behavior>()`.\n203\t- Keep tests small: arrange, act, assert.\n204\t\n205\t```python\n206\tdef test_load_config(tmp_path: pathlib.Path) -> None:\n207\t    \"\"\"Verify config loads with expected values.\"\"\"\n208\t    # Arrange\n209\t    config_file = tmp_path / \"config.txt\"\n210\t    config_file.write_text(\"setting=value\\n\")\n211\t    # Act\n212\t    result = load_config(config_file)\n213\t    # Assert\n214\t    assert result[\"setting\"] == \"value\"\n215\t```\n216\t\n217\t### Useful Commands\n218\t\n219\t```bash\n220\thatch run test:test                              # Full suite\n221\thatch run test:test tests/test_context.py        # Specific file\n222\thatch run test:test --log-level DEBUG            # Verbose output\n223\thatch run test:coverage-report                   # Coverage summary\n224\t```\n225\t\n226\t---\n227\t\n228\t## Commit Guidelines\n229\t\n230\tFromager follows [Conventional Commits](https://www.conventionalcommits.org/en/v1.0.0/).\n231\t\n232\t```text\n233\t<type>(<scope>): <short summary>\n234\t\n235\t<body explaining what + why>\n236\t\n237\t<footer for metadata>\n238\t```\n239\t\n240\t### Common Types\n241\t\n242\t- `feat`: new functionality\n243\t- `fix`: bug fix\n244\t- `docs`: documentation only\n245\t- `test`: tests only\n246\t- `refactor`: behavioral no-op refactor\n247\t- `perf`: performance improvement\n248\t- `chore`: tooling or dependency change\n249\t\n250\t### Writing Tips\n251\t\n252\t- Subject ≤ 72 characters, imperative voice, no trailing period.\n253\t- Describe motivation in the body if the diff is not self-explanatory.\n254\t- Reference issues when relevant (`Closes: #123`).\n255\t\n256\t### Examples\n257\t\n258\t```text\n259\tfeat(http_retry): add exponential backoff with jitter\n260\t\n261\tSupport transient failures more gracefully by adding jittered backoff.\n262\t\n263\tCloses: #456\n264\t```\n265\t\n266\t```text\n267\tfix(constraints): handle missing constraint file gracefully\n268\t\n269\tValidate file existence and emit a helpful message instead of crashing.\n270\t```\n271\t\n272\t### AI-Generated Code Attribution\n273\t\n274\tWhen AI tools create or significantly modify code, add attribution:\n275\t\n276\t```text\n277\tfeat(resolver): add exponential backoff for HTTP retries\n278\t\n279\tImproves resilience when PyPI is under load by adding jittered backoff.\n280\t\n281\tCo-Authored-By: Claude <claude@anthropic.com>\n282\tCloses: #456\n283\t```\n284\t\n285\tAvoid vague messages like `fix bug`, `update files`, or `WIP`.\n286\t\n287\t---\n288\t\n289\t## Before Submitting\n290\t\n291\t### Quality Checklist\n292\t\n293\t- [ ] `hatch run lint:fix`\n294\t- [ ] `hatch run test:test`\n295\t- [ ] `hatch run mypy:check`\n296\t- [ ] `hatch run lint:check`\n297\t\n298\t### Code Review Checklist\n299\t\n300\t- [ ] Every function is fully typed.\n301\t- [ ] Public APIs include docstrings.\n302\t- [ ] Tests cover the change (positive + edge cases).\n303\t- [ ] No trailing whitespace; files end with a newline.\n304\t- [ ] Conventional Commit format followed.\n305\t- [ ] Code aligns with existing patterns.\n306\t\n307\t---\n308\t\n309\t## Design Patterns Used in Fromager\n310\t\n311\t### Context Managers\n312\t\n313\t```python\n314\t@contextlib.contextmanager\n315\tdef resource_context() -> typing.Generator[None, None, None]:\n316\t    \"\"\"Manage resource lifecycle.\"\"\"\n317\t    setup_resource()\n318\t    try:\n319\t        yield\n320\t    finally:\n321\t        cleanup_resource()\n322\t```\n323\t\n324\t### Thread-Safe Decorators\n325\t\n326\t```python\n327\tdef with_thread_lock() -> typing.Callable:\n328\t    \"\"\"Create thread-safe wrapper.\"\"\"\n329\t    lock = threading.Lock()\n330\t\n331\t    def decorator(func: typing.Callable) -> typing.Callable:\n332\t        @functools.wraps(func)\n333\t        def wrapper(*args: typing.Any, **kwargs: typing.Any) -> typing.Any:\n334\t            with lock:\n335\t                return func(*args, **kwargs)\n336\t        return wrapper\n337\t    return decorator\n338\t```\n339\t\n340\t### Pydantic Validation\n341\t\n342\t```python\n343\tdef _validate_envkey(value: typing.Any) -> str:\n344\t    \"\"\"Normalize environment variable keys.\"\"\"\n345\t    if isinstance(value, bool):\n346\t        return \"1\" if value else \"0\"\n347\t    if isinstance(value, (int, float)):\n348\t        return str(value)\n349\t    if isinstance(value, str):\n350\t        return value.strip()\n351\t    raise TypeError(f\"unsupported type {type(value)}: {value!r}\")\n352\t\n353\tEnvKey = typing.Annotated[str, BeforeValidator(_validate_envkey)]\n354\t```\n355\t\n356\t---\n357\t\n358\t## Quick Reference\n359\t\n360\t### Commands\n361\t\n362\t| Task | Command |\n363\t| -- | -- |\n364\t| Run tests | `hatch run test:test` |\n365\t| Check code quality | `hatch run lint:check` |\n366\t| Fix formatting | `hatch run lint:fix` |\n367\t| Type checking | `hatch run mypy:check` |\n368\t\n369\t### Standards\n370\t\n371\t| Standard | Requirement |\n372\t| -- | -- |\n373\t| Type annotations | Required for every function |\n374\t| Docstrings | Required on public APIs |\n375\t| Tests | Required for new behavior |\n376\t| Trailing whitespace | Forbidden |\n377\t| File endings | Single newline |\n378\t| Commit format | Conventional Commits |\n379\t\n380\t---\n381\t\n382\t## Getting Help\n383\t\n384\t- Open an issue for discussion or questions or design proposals.\n385\t- File an issue with a minimal reproduction for bugs.\n386\t- Ask for targeted feedback directly in your pull request.\n387\t\n388\t## Resources\n389\t\n390\t- [PEP 8 - Style Guide](https://www.python.org/dev/peps/pep-0008/)\n391\t- [PEP 484 - Type Hints](https://www.python.org/dev/peps/pep-0484/)\n392\t- [Conventional Commits](https://www.conventionalcommits.org/)\n393\t- [pytest Documentation](https://docs.pytest.org/)\n394\t- [Ruff Documentation](https://docs.astral.sh/ruff/)\n395\t\n396\t---\n397\t\n398\t**Thanks for contributing to Fromager!** If you spot gaps or have suggestions for this guide, open an issue or start a discussion—we love improving our contributor experience.\n399\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"13ec0ed9-053c-412d-a45d-9f05a42f56e3","uuid":"c2797943-5d16-4003-8f1a-a5fb9753057e","timestamp":"2026-10-09T17:10:44.245Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/alustosa/git/model-routing-eval/.work/agent-eval/2026-10-09-claude-haiku-5-5-effort-medium-r1/cases/r01_fromager_distinfo/CONTRIBUTING.md","content":"# Contributing to Fromager\n\nFromager thrives on practical, well-tested contributions. This guide summarizes how to set up a workspace, follow our standards, and submit polished changes. Skim it once, keep it handy, and refer back whenever you are unsure.\n\n> **Note**: If you're using AI coding assistants, also see [AGENTS.md](AGENTS.md) for AI-optimized quick reference.\n\n## Table of Contents\n\n- [Quick Start](#quick-start)\n- [Coding Standards](#coding-standards)\n- [Testing](#testing)\n- [Commit Guidelines](#commit-guidelines)\n- [Before Submitting](#before-submitting)\n- [Design Patterns Used in Fromager](#design-patterns-used-in-fromager)\n- [Quick Reference](#quick-reference)\n- [Getting Help](#getting-help)\n- [Resources](#resources)\n\n---\n\n## Quick Start\n\n### Prerequisites\n\n- Python 3.12 or newer\n\n- `hatch` for environment and task management\n\n  ```bash\n  pip install hatch\n  # or\n  pipx install hatch  # recommended\n  ```\n\n### Initial Setup\n\n```bash\n# 1. Fork the repository on GitHub\n# 2. Clone your fork\ngit clone https://github.com/<your-username>/fromager.git\ncd fromager\n\n# 3. Add upstream remote\ngit remote add upstream https://github.com/python-wheel-build/fromager.git\n\n# 4. Create development environment\nhatch env create\n\n# 5. Install pre-commit hooks (optional but recommended)\nhatch run lint:install-hooks\n```\n\n### Contribution Workflow\n\n```bash\n# 1. Sync with upstream\ngit checkout main\ngit fetch upstream\ngit merge upstream/main\n\n# 2. Create a feature branch\ngit checkout -b feat/<short-description>\n\n# 3. Make changes and test as you go\nhatch run test:test tests/test_<module>.py       # Test your specific changes\n\n# 4. Before committing, run full quality checks\nhatch run lint:precommit\n\n# 5. Commit using Conventional Commits\ngit commit -m \"feat(scope): short summary\"\n\n# 6. Push to your fork\ngit push origin feat/<short-description>\n\n# 7. Create a pull request on GitHub\n```\n\n### Pre-commit Hook (Recommended)\n\nTo ensure quality checks run automatically before each commit, install the pre-commit hooks that run linting, mypy type checking, and commit message validation:\n\n```bash\n# Install pre-commit hooks (run once after cloning)\nhatch run lint:install-hooks\n\n# The hooks automatically run linters before each commit and validate\n# that commit messages follow Conventional Commits format.\n\n# If a hook fails, it will prevent the commit and show helpful messages.\n# You can fix formatting issues automatically with:\nhatch run lint:fix\n```\n\nThe pre-commit hooks prevent commits that would fail CI quality checks, and enforce that all commit messages follow the [Conventional Commits](https://www.conventionalcommits.org/) format documented in the [Commit Guidelines](#commit-guidelines) section.\n\n---\n\n## Coding Standards\n\n### Type Annotations\n\n- Every function (including tests) must annotate all parameters and return values.\n- Use modern `X | None` syntax instead of `Optional[X]` (PEP 604).\n- Prefer precise collection types (`list[str]`, `dict[str, int]`, etc.).\n\n```python\ndef process(data: str | None, count: int = 0) -> dict[str, int]:\n    \"\"\"Process the input and return aggregate counts.\"\"\"\n    return {}\n```\n\n### Code Quality and Formatting\n\n- Ruff enforces both formatting and linting.\n- Run `hatch run lint:fix` to automatically format code.\n- See [Quick Reference](#quick-reference) for additional commands.\n\n### Import Organization\n\n- **PEP 8: imports should be at the top**: All import statements must be placed at the top of the file, after module docstrings and before other code.\n- **No local imports**: Do not place import statements inside functions, methods, or conditional blocks.\n\n### Documentation Expectations\n\n- Add a module docstring describing purpose and high-level behavior.\n- Public functions and classes require docstrings that cover arguments, return values, and noteworthy behavior.\n- Keep prose short and imperative; explain \"why\" decisions when the code itself cannot.\n\n```python\ndef retry_on_exception(\n    exceptions: tuple[type[Exception], ...],\n    max_attempts: int = 5,\n) -> typing.Callable:\n    \"\"\"Retry decorated call on the provided exception types.\n\n    Args:\n        exceptions: Exception types that trigger a retry.\n        max_attempts: Maximum number of attempts.\n\n    Returns:\n        Decorator function.\n    \"\"\"\n    ...\n```\n\n### Commenting Guidelines\n\n- Write self-explanatory code; reserve comments for non-obvious intent or domain context.\n- Capture reasoning, invariants, or unexpected trade-offs—never repeat the code literally.\n\n```python\n# Bad - comment just repeats the code\nx = x + 1  # increment x\n\n# Good - clear variable name makes the comment unnecessary\ntotal_attempts += 1\n\n# Good - comment explains the reasoning behind the approach\n# Exponential backoff avoids thundering herd when many jobs retry at once.\nwait_time = min(2**attempt + random.uniform(0, 1), max_backoff)\n```\n\n### Logging\n\n- Use a module-level logger and the appropriate log level for the situation.\n- When processing per-requirement work, wrap nested calls in `req_ctxvar_context()` so log records automatically include the package (and optional version). This keeps CLI logs searchable even when work runs in parallel.\n\n```python\nlogger = logging.getLogger(__name__)\n\ndef sync_artifacts() -> None:\n    logger.info(\"Starting artifact sync\")\n    logger.debug(\"Artifacts queued: %s\", pending_jobs)\n\n# When processing a specific package, wrap calls to include package info in logs\n# req: Requirement object for the package being processed\n# version: Version string being built (optional)\nwith req_ctxvar_context(req, version):\n    logger.info(\"Resolving build dependencies\")  # Logs will include package name\n```\n\n### Error Handling\n\n- Raise specific exceptions with actionable messages.\n- Chain exceptions (`raise ... from e`) so stack traces stay informative.\n\n```python\ntry:\n    result = process_data(file_path)\nexcept FileNotFoundError as err:\n    raise ValueError(f\"Cannot load config at {file_path}\") from err\n```\n\n---\n\n## Testing\n\n### Structure\n\n- Place tests under `tests/`.\n- Name files `test_<module>.py` and functions `test_<behavior>()`.\n- Keep tests small: arrange, act, assert.\n\n```python\ndef test_load_config(tmp_path: pathlib.Path) -> None:\n    \"\"\"Verify config loads with expected values.\"\"\"\n    # Arrange\n    config_file = tmp_path / \"config.txt\"\n    config_file.write_text(\"setting=value\\n\")\n    # Act\n    result = load_config(config_file)\n    # Assert\n    assert result[\"setting\"] == \"value\"\n```\n\n### Useful Commands\n\n```bash\nhatch run test:test                              # Full suite\nhatch run test:test tests/test_context.py        # Specific file\nhatch run test:test --log-level DEBUG            # Verbose output\nhatch run test:coverage-report                   # Coverage summary\n```\n\n---\n\n## Commit Guidelines\n\nFromager follows [Conventional Commits](https://www.conventionalcommits.org/en/v1.0.0/).\n\n```text\n<type>(<scope>): <short summary>\n\n<body explaining what + why>\n\n<footer for metadata>\n```\n\n### Common Types\n\n- `feat`: new functionality\n- `fix`: bug fix\n- `docs`: documentation only\n- `test`: tests only\n- `refactor`: behavioral no-op refactor\n- `perf`: performance improvement\n- `chore`: tooling or dependency change\n\n### Writing Tips\n\n- Subject ≤ 72 characters, imperative voice, no trailing period.\n- Describe motivation in the body if the diff is not self-explanatory.\n- Reference issues when relevant (`Closes: #123`).\n\n### Examples\n\n```text\nfeat(http_retry): add exponential backoff with jitter\n\nSupport transient failures more gracefully by adding jittered backoff.\n\nCloses: #456\n```\n\n```text\nfix(constraints): handle missing constraint file gracefully\n\nValidate file existence and emit a helpful message instead of crashing.\n```\n\n### AI-Generated Code Attribution\n\nWhen AI tools create or significantly modify code, add attribution:\n\n```text\nfeat(resolver): add exponential backoff for HTTP retries\n\nImproves resilience when PyPI is under load by adding jittered backoff.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude <claude@anthropic.com>\nCloses: #456\n```\n\nAvoid vague messages like `fix bug`, `update files`, or `WIP`.\n\n---\n\n## Before Submitting\n\n### Quality Checklist\n\n- [ ] `hatch run lint:fix`\n- [ ] `hatch run test:test`\n- [ ] `hatch run mypy:check`\n- [ ] `hatch run lint:check`\n\n### Code Review Checklist\n\n- [ ] Every function is fully typed.\n- [ ] Public APIs include docstrings.\n- [ ] Tests cover the change (positive + edge cases).\n- [ ] No trailing whitespace; files end with a newline.\n- [ ] Conventional Commit format followed.\n- [ ] Code aligns with existing patterns.\n\n---\n\n## Design Patterns Used in Fromager\n\n### Context Managers\n\n```python\n@contextlib.contextmanager\ndef resource_context() -> typing.Generator[None, None, None]:\n    \"\"\"Manage resource lifecycle.\"\"\"\n    setup_resource()\n    try:\n        yield\n    finally:\n        cleanup_resource()\n```\n\n### Thread-Safe Decorators\n\n```python\ndef with_thread_lock() -> typing.Callable:\n    \"\"\"Create thread-safe wrapper.\"\"\"\n    lock = threading.Lock()\n\n    def decorator(func: typing.Callable) -> typing.Callable:\n        @functools.wraps(func)\n        def wrapper(*args: typing.Any, **kwargs: typing.Any) -> typing.Any:\n            with lock:\n                return func(*args, **kwargs)\n        return wrapper\n    return decorator\n```\n\n### Pydantic Validation\n\n```python\ndef _validate_envkey(value: typing.Any) -> str:\n    \"\"\"Normalize environment variable keys.\"\"\"\n    if isinstance(value, bool):\n        return \"1\" if value else \"0\"\n    if isinstance(value, (int, float)):\n        return str(value)\n    if isinstance(value, str):\n        return value.strip()\n    raise TypeError(f\"unsupported type {type(value)}: {value!r}\")\n\nEnvKey = typing.Annotated[str, BeforeValidator(_validate_envkey)]\n```\n\n---\n\n## Quick Reference\n\n### Commands\n\n| Task | Command |\n| -- | -- |\n| Run tests | `hatch run test:test` |\n| Check code quality | `hatch run lint:check` |\n| Fix formatting | `hatch run lint:fix` |\n| Type checking | `hatch run mypy:check` |\n\n### Standards\n\n| Standard | Requirement |\n| -- | -- |\n| Type annotations | Required for every function |\n| Docstrings | Required on public APIs |\n| Tests | Required for new behavior |\n| Trailing whitespace | Forbidden |\n| File endings | Single newline |\n| Commit format | Conventional Commits |\n\n---\n\n## Getting Help\n\n- Open an issue for discussion or questions or design proposals.\n- File an issue with a minimal reproduction for bugs.\n- Ask for targeted feedback directly in your pull request.\n\n## Resources\n\n- [PEP 8 - Style Guide](https://www.python.org/dev/peps/pep-0008/)\n- [PEP 484 - Type Hints](https://www.python.org/dev/peps/pep-0484/)\n- [Conventional Commits](https://www.conventionalcommits.org/)\n- [pytest Documentation](https://docs.pytest.org/)\n- [Ruff Documentation](https://docs.astral.sh/ruff/)\n\n---\n\n**Thanks for contributing to Fromager!** If you spot gaps or have suggestions for this guide, open an issue or start a discussion—we love improving our contributor experience.\n","numLines":399,"startLine":1,"totalLines":399}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01HVejuahAe69zFr48MgYDJx","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
